# Production reverse proxy. SITE_ADDRESS comes from the compose service's
# environment (set it in .env, e.g. app.example.com). Caddy obtains and
# renews the Let's Encrypt certificate automatically for any real domain.
#
# Two deliberate properties, documented so they aren't "fixed" casually:
#
# 1. Only the frontend is routed. The Next.js BFF proxies every product
#    request server-side, so while Stripe runs against the fake client
#    there is no legitimate external caller of the backend at all — not
#    routing it removes the forged-webhook surface entirely.
#
#    This stays closed by default even now that RealStripeClient exists,
#    because the two are not the same decision: the SDK ships in the image
#    and the client is selected by STRIPE_API_KEY, but a deployment still
#    on the fake gains nothing from a publicly reachable webhook receiver
#    and would be handing out an unauthenticated POST surface for free.
#
#    Uncomment this block at the same time you set STRIPE_API_KEY — the two
#    go together, and neither is useful alone (Stripe cannot deliver events
#    to an unrouted endpoint, and an unconfigured deployment does not want
#    the route). See docs/11-production-deployment.md §"Turning on real
#    Stripe billing".
#
#	handle /webhooks/stripe* {
#		reverse_proxy backend:8000
#	}
#
#    The receiver is not left unprotected by this: POST /webhooks/stripe
#    verifies Stripe's own `t=...,v1=...` HMAC over the raw body and
#    enforces a 5-minute replay window before touching the database
#    (app/services/stripe_client.py). Routing decides who can reach it;
#    the signature decides who is believed.
#
# 2. No trusted_proxies directive. Caddy's default DISCARDS any
#    client-supplied X-Forwarded-For and sets its own — that spoof-safety
#    is what makes the forwarded IP trustworthy for the register rate
#    limiter and the legally-significant e-signature capture. Only add
#    trusted_proxies if you place another load balancer IN FRONT of Caddy.

{$SITE_ADDRESS} {
	encode gzip

	# TLS-only header — deliberately set here, not in next.config.ts: HSTS
	# is meaningless without TLS and harmful if emitted over dev HTTP.
	header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"

	# Outer belt for upload abuse; the application-layer caps
	# (app/core/uploads.py) are the real per-route limits.
	request_body {
		max_size 30MB
	}

	reverse_proxy frontend:3000
}
